Band match (dcsvr_band_match, dcsvr_band_match_color, dcsvr_band_stats; two layers only; native/quadviews/dcsvr_bandmatch.h,
dcsvr_bandmatch_cs.hlsl, dcsvr_twolayer_ps.hlsl, dcsvr_live.h). The fovea's faint flash at its border when the eyes move
comes from any static difference between the focus image and the periphery in the edge band: the band moves over the
scene with the gaze and turns each point it crosses from one look to the other. A brightness or tint difference (DLSS 5
on the focus views only, which Cheeky's Match brightness evens out only on average over the whole DLSS 5 area; another
DLSS preset; a per-view difference of DCS's own) is the one a single gain can remove. Once per eye and frame, before the
eye's focus pass, one 16 x 16 compute group samples the band (32 x 32 grid, weight 4 a (1 - a)) and compares the focus at
the periphery's resolution (the edge match's tent at full width) with the periphery in the same direction; the weighted
mean log ratio is the target gain (luminance, or per colour channel with dcsvr_band_match_color=1), within 1 +- 0.1,
approached at 0.05 per frame, snapped on the first measurement, after a scene cut and when the mode changes. The focus
pass multiplies the focus colour by gain^dcsvr_band_match (0..1; 0, the default, changes nothing: the pass is the one
before). dcsvr_band_stats=1 measures without the gain. Both write a log line every 30 s ("DCSVR band match ..."), per
eye the measured ratio periphery/focus, its spread over time, the local mismatch (the spread of the ratio over the band:
what one gain cannot remove) and the gain. All three keys are live. The gain sits in t4 and
dcsvrDebug.zw of the focus pass (the constant buffer keeps its size).
